(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("ceph",{"ceph/what-ceph-is-rados-and-the-architecture":{"html":"\u003cp>Software-defined storage means the intelligence lives in software that runs on ordinary servers, not in a proprietary array. Ceph is the best known open source example, and it powers many OpenStack and Kubernetes clusters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What software-defined storage means\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RADOS and the three services on top\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The daemons and their jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When Ceph is a good fit and when it is not\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"software-defined-storage\">Software-defined storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A traditional array puts disks, controllers and software in one box. A software-defined system runs on standard servers, keeps several copies of data across them, and heals itself when a disk or server fails. You scale by adding servers, and there is no single controller to fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rados-and-the-services\">RADOS and the services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RADOS\u003c/strong> is the reliable distributed object store at the bottom. Everything is stored as objects placed by an algorithm, not by a central table. Three services sit on top:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Service\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What you get\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>RBD\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Block devices\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VM disks, Kubernetes volumes, databases\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>CephFS\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A POSIX shared file system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Home directories, shared data, RWX volumes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>RGW\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>S3 and Swift object storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backups, artifacts, media\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-daemons\">The daemons\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Daemon\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Count\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>MON\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3 or 5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keep the cluster map and agree on it by quorum\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>MGR\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 or more\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Metrics, dashboard and orchestration modules\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>OSD\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One per disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Store data, replicate, recover\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>MDS\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 or more per file system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Serve CephFS metadata\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>RGW\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 or more\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Serve the S3 and Swift APIs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"rbd&gt;rados;fs&gt;rados;rgw&gt;rados;rados&gt;mon;rados&gt;osd\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Ceph services on one object store\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rbd\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RBD block\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fs\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CephFS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rgw\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RGW S3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rados\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RADOS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Monitors\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"osd\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSDs on disks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, RBD block to RADOS; 2, CephFS to RADOS; 3, RGW S3 to RADOS; 4, RADOS to Monitors; 5, RADOS to OSDs on disks.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rbd\">\u003cdt>RBD block \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Block devices for VMs and containers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One slow OSD can slow many images.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fs\">\u003cdt>CephFS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A shared POSIX file system.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An overloaded MDS stalls metadata operations.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rgw\">\u003cdt>RGW S3 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An S3 compatible object gateway.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large buckets need index sharding.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rados\">\u003cdt>RADOS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The object store that places and protects data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Everything above depends on it being healthy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Monitors \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Hold the cluster map and form a quorum.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Losing quorum stops the cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"osd\">\u003cdt>OSDs on disks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Store objects and repair each other.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many down at once makes data unavailable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fit\">Fit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Ceph suits clusters of at least three nodes (preferably more) where you want one platform for several storage types and can operate it. It is a poor fit for a single server, a very small team with no time to learn it, or workloads that need the lowest possible latency from one local disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-storage-teams-one-bill\">Incident: Three storage teams, one bill\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A company paid for three separate storage systems.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  cluster:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    health: HEALTH_OK\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  services:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    mon: 3 daemons, quorum a,b,c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    osd: 36 osds: 36 up, 36 in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    rgw: 2 daemons active\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The consolidated cluster served VM disks, shared files and S3 buckets from 12 servers. The team learned one platform, added capacity by adding servers, and retired the old arrays as support contracts ended.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running Ceph on a single node and calling it highly available\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating it as a drop in replacement for a local disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the network, which carries all replication traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting without anyone who will operate it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"software-defined-storage","text":"Software-defined storage"},{"id":"rados-and-the-services","text":"RADOS and the services"},{"id":"the-daemons","text":"The daemons"},{"id":"fit","text":"Fit"},{"id":"incident-three-storage-teams-one-bill","text":"Incident: Three storage teams, one bill"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ceph architecture","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/architecture/"},{"t":"Ceph documentation","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/"},{"t":"Ceph introduction","u":"https://ceph.io/en/discover/"}],"text":"Software-defined storage means the intelligence lives in software that runs on ordinary servers, not in a proprietary array. Ceph is the best known open source example, and it powers many OpenStack and Kubernetes clusters. What you will learn What software-defined storage means RADOS and the three services on top The daemons and their jobs When Ceph is a good fit and when it is not Software-defined storage A traditional array puts disks, controllers and software in one box. A software-defined system runs on standard servers, keeps several copies of data across them, and heals itself when a disk or server fails. You scale by adding servers, and there is no single controller to fail. RADOS and the services RADOS is the reliable distributed object store at the bottom. Everything is stored as objects placed by an algorithm, not by a central table. Three services sit on top: Service What you get Typical use RBD Block devices VM disks, Kubernetes volumes, databases CephFS A POSIX shared file system Home directories, shared data, RWX volumes RGW S3 and Swift object storage Backups, artifacts, media The daemons Daemon Count Job MON 3 or 5 Keep the cluster map and agree on it by quorum MGR 2 or more Metrics, dashboard and orchestration modules OSD One per disk Store data, replicate, recover MDS 2 or more per file system Serve CephFS metadata RGW 2 or more Serve the S3 and Swift APIs Ceph services on one object store Flow in order: 1, RBD block to RADOS; 2, CephFS to RADOS; 3, RGW S3 to RADOS; 4, RADOS to Monitors; 5, RADOS to OSDs on disks. Service Data store Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. RBD block Service What it does: Block devices for VMs and containers. If it fails or is weak: One slow OSD can slow many images. CephFS Service What it does: A shared POSIX file system. If it fails or is weak: An overloaded MDS stalls metadata operations. RGW S3 Service What it does: An S3 compatible object gateway. If it fails or is weak: Large buckets need index sharding. RADOS Data store What it does: The object store that places and protects data. If it fails or is weak: Everything above depends on it being healthy. Monitors Security What it does: Hold the cluster map and form a quorum. If it fails or is weak: Losing quorum stops the cluster. OSDs on disks Data store What it does: Store objects and repair each other. If it fails or is weak: Too many down at once makes data unavailable. Fit Ceph suits clusters of at least three nodes (preferably more) where you want one platform for several storage types and can operate it. It is a poor fit for a single server, a very small team with no time to learn it, or workloads that need the lowest possible latency from one local disk. Incident: Three storage teams, one bill Incident A company paid for three separate storage systems. $ ceph -s cluster: health: HEALTH_OK services: mon: 3 daemons, quorum a,b,c osd: 36 osds: 36 up, 36 in rgw: 2 daemons active The consolidated cluster served VM disks, shared files and S3 buckets from 12 servers. The team learned one platform, added capacity by adding servers, and retired the old arrays as support contracts ended. Common mistakes Running Ceph on a single node and calling it highly available Treating it as a drop in replacement for a local disk Ignoring the network, which carries all replication traffic Starting without anyone who will operate it"},"ceph/monitors-managers-osds-and-quorum":{"html":"\u003cp>Four words explain most Ceph status output: quorum, up, down and in. Learn what each one means and what BlueStore does under an OSD.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Quorum and why monitors come in odd numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Manager and its modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>OSD states: up, down, in and out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>BlueStore, the DB and WAL devices\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quorum\">Quorum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Monitors keep the authoritative cluster map: who the monitors and OSDs are, where they are, and how data is placed. They agree on changes by majority vote. With three monitors you can lose one; with five you can lose two. Two monitors tolerate nothing, since losing one leaves no majority. Without quorum clients can no longer find data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managers\">Managers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>manager\u003c/strong> daemon collects metrics, runs the dashboard and Prometheus exporter, and hosts modules such as the PG autoscaler and the orchestrator. Run at least two: one active, one standby.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"osd-states\">OSD states\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An OSD has two independent states. \u003cstrong>Up or down\u003c/strong> says whether the daemon is running and talking. \u003cstrong>In or out\u003c/strong> says whether it takes part in data placement. A crashed OSD becomes down; after a timeout (10 minutes by default) it is marked out, and the cluster starts re-creating its data elsewhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"up&gt;down;down&gt;out;out&gt;rec;down&gt;up\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What happens when an OSD fails\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"12.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"up\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSD up and in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"down\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSD down\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSD out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rec\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Recovery and backfill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, OSD up and in to OSD down; 2, OSD down to OSD out; 3, OSD out to Recovery and backfill; 4, OSD down to OSD up and in.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"up\">\u003cdt>OSD up and in \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves reads and writes for its placement groups.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hardware errors may appear before it fails.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"down\">\u003cdt>OSD down \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The daemon stopped answering; data is still on the disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Short outages heal quickly when it returns.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>OSD out \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After the timeout the cluster stops waiting for it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Recovery now copies data to other OSDs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rec\">\u003cdt>Recovery and backfill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Other OSDs rebuild the missing copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heavy recovery traffic can slow clients.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bluestore\">BlueStore\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>BlueStore manages the raw device itself. Object data goes to the main device; metadata goes to RocksDB; a write-ahead log makes writes safe. For hard drives, place the DB and WAL on an SSD or NVMe to speed up metadata and small writes. Size the DB at roughly 1 to 4 percent of the data device.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s\nceph osd tree\nceph osd stat\nceph mon stat\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph -s\nceph osd tree\nceph osd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>\nceph mon \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-lost-quorum-in-one-rack\">Incident: Lost quorum in one rack\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A power cut stopped all storage IO.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  cluster:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    health: HEALTH_ERR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">            1/3 mons down, quorum a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  services:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    mon: 3 daemons, quorum a (age 4m)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When the power dropped, two monitors failed together because they shared a rack. The one survivor could not form a majority. After power returned, the team spread monitors across three racks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running an even number of monitors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placing all monitors in one failure domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing down with out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting DB and WAL for many OSDs on one small SSD\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"quorum","text":"Quorum"},{"id":"managers","text":"Managers"},{"id":"osd-states","text":"OSD states"},{"id":"bluestore","text":"BlueStore"},{"id":"incident-lost-quorum-in-one-rack","text":"Incident: Lost quorum in one rack"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Monitor configuration","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/configuration/mon-config-ref/"},{"t":"BlueStore configuration","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/configuration/bluestore-config-ref/"},{"t":"Ceph operations","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/"}],"text":"Four words explain most Ceph status output: quorum, up, down and in. Learn what each one means and what BlueStore does under an OSD. What you will learn Quorum and why monitors come in odd numbers Manager and its modules OSD states: up, down, in and out BlueStore, the DB and WAL devices Quorum Monitors keep the authoritative cluster map: who the monitors and OSDs are, where they are, and how data is placed. They agree on changes by majority vote. With three monitors you can lose one; with five you can lose two. Two monitors tolerate nothing, since losing one leaves no majority. Without quorum clients can no longer find data. Managers The manager daemon collects metrics, runs the dashboard and Prometheus exporter, and hosts modules such as the PG autoscaler and the orchestrator. Run at least two: one active, one standby. OSD states An OSD has two independent states. Up or down says whether the daemon is running and talking. In or out says whether it takes part in data placement. A crashed OSD becomes down; after a timeout (10 minutes by default) it is marked out, and the cluster starts re-creating its data elsewhere. What happens when an OSD fails Flow in order: 1, OSD up and in to OSD down; 2, OSD down to OSD out; 3, OSD out to Recovery and backfill; 4, OSD down to OSD up and in. Data store Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. OSD up and in Data store What it does: Serves reads and writes for its placement groups. If it fails or is weak: Hardware errors may appear before it fails. OSD down Observability What it does: The daemon stopped answering; data is still on the disk. If it fails or is weak: Short outages heal quickly when it returns. OSD out Step What it does: After the timeout the cluster stops waiting for it. If it fails or is weak: Recovery now copies data to other OSDs. Recovery and backfill Step What it does: Other OSDs rebuild the missing copies. If it fails or is weak: Heavy recovery traffic can slow clients. BlueStore BlueStore manages the raw device itself. Object data goes to the main device; metadata goes to RocksDB; a write-ahead log makes writes safe. For hard drives, place the DB and WAL on an SSD or NVMe to speed up metadata and small writes. Size the DB at roughly 1 to 4 percent of the data device. ceph -s ceph osd tree ceph osd stat ceph mon stat Incident: Lost quorum in one rack Incident A power cut stopped all storage IO. $ ceph -s cluster: health: HEALTH_ERR 1/3 mons down, quorum a services: mon: 3 daemons, quorum a (age 4m) When the power dropped, two monitors failed together because they shared a rack. The one survivor could not form a majority. After power returned, the team spread monitors across three racks. Common mistakes Running an even number of monitors Placing all monitors in one failure domain Confusing down with out Putting DB and WAL for many OSDs on one small SSD"},"ceph/crush-pools-and-placement-groups":{"html":"\u003cp>CRUSH is why Ceph scales. Clients compute where data lives instead of asking a central server, so there is no lookup bottleneck. The price is that you must understand failure domains.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The path from object to OSD\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure domains and CRUSH rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pools, replica count and min_size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Placement group counts and the autoscaler\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"from-object-to-osd\">From object to OSD\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>An object is written into a \u003cstrong>pool\u003c/strong> under a name.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Its name is hashed to a \u003cstrong>placement group\u003c/strong> within the pool.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CRUSH\u003c/strong> maps that placement group to a list of OSDs, honouring the rule&#39;s failure domain.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The first OSD in the list is the primary; it forwards writes to the others.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Because CRUSH is a calculation over the cluster map, any client can do it and no one stores a per object table.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"obj&gt;pool;pool&gt;pg;pg&gt;crush;crush&gt;osds\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where does an object go?\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obj\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Placement group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"crush\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CRUSH rule\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"osds\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSDs on hosts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Object to Pool; 2, Pool to Placement group; 3, Placement group to CRUSH rule; 4, CRUSH rule to OSDs on hosts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obj\">\u003cdt>Object \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Has a name and data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Very large objects can slow recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>Pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sets replica count, rule and PG number.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong settings are costly to change later.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pg\">\u003cdt>Placement group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A bucket of objects that moves as a unit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too few PGs gives uneven data spread.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"crush\">\u003cdt>CRUSH rule \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Picks OSDs in different failure domains.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A rule on the wrong domain risks correlated loss.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"osds\">\u003cdt>OSDs on hosts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The disks that hold the copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unequal weights cause hot spots.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-domains\">Failure domains\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A CRUSH rule says &quot;choose N distinct buckets of type host&quot; or rack, room or datacenter. With three replicas and host failure domain, each copy is on a different server. With rack, each is in a different rack and a whole rack can fail. You need enough buckets: three racks for three copies by rack.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pools\">Pools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph osd pool create vms 128\nceph osd pool set vms size 3\nceph osd pool set vms min_size 2\nceph osd pool application enable vms rbd\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph osd pool create vms 128\nceph osd pool \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> vms size 3\nceph osd pool \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> vms min_size 2\nceph osd pool application \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> vms rbd\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>size\u003c/code> is the number of copies. \u003ccode>min_size\u003c/code> is how many must be available to accept IO. With size 3 and min_size 2, the pool keeps working with one copy missing and stops at two missing, protecting against writing data that exists only once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"placement-groups\">Placement groups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each OSD should carry about 100 PGs. Too few makes data uneven; too many wastes memory. Leave the \u003cstrong>PG autoscaler\u003c/strong> on, and set a \u003ccode>target_size_ratio\u003c/code> for pools you know will grow.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-copies-on-one-host\">Incident: Two copies on one host\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Losing a server took some data offline.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph osd crush rule dump replicated_rule | grep type\nceph health detail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd crush rule dump replicated_rule | grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">                &quot;type&quot;: &quot;osd&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health detail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_ERR 12 pgs inactive; 12 pgs stuck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pool used a rule with an OSD failure domain, so two of three copies of some PGs shared a server. When that server failed, the PGs dropped below min_size. A host failure domain rule fixed the placement and data moved apart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default rule without checking its failure domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting min_size to 1\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating pools with a fixed PG count and never revisiting it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a rack failure domain with fewer racks than copies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"from-object-to-osd","text":"From object to OSD"},{"id":"failure-domains","text":"Failure domains"},{"id":"pools","text":"Pools"},{"id":"placement-groups","text":"Placement groups"},{"id":"incident-two-copies-on-one-host","text":"Incident: Two copies on one host"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CRUSH maps","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/crush-map/"},{"t":"Placement groups","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/placement-groups/"},{"t":"Pools","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/pools/"}],"text":"CRUSH is why Ceph scales. Clients compute where data lives instead of asking a central server, so there is no lookup bottleneck. The price is that you must understand failure domains. What you will learn The path from object to OSD Failure domains and CRUSH rules Pools, replica count and min_size Placement group counts and the autoscaler From object to OSD An object is written into a pool under a name. Its name is hashed to a placement group within the pool. CRUSH maps that placement group to a list of OSDs, honouring the rule&#39;s failure domain. The first OSD in the list is the primary; it forwards writes to the others. Because CRUSH is a calculation over the cluster map, any client can do it and no one stores a per object table. Where does an object go? Flow in order: 1, Object to Pool; 2, Pool to Placement group; 3, Placement group to CRUSH rule; 4, CRUSH rule to OSDs on hosts. Data store Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Object Data store What it does: Has a name and data. If it fails or is weak: Very large objects can slow recovery. Pool Step What it does: Sets replica count, rule and PG number. If it fails or is weak: Wrong settings are costly to change later. Placement group Step What it does: A bucket of objects that moves as a unit. If it fails or is weak: Too few PGs gives uneven data spread. CRUSH rule Security What it does: Picks OSDs in different failure domains. If it fails or is weak: A rule on the wrong domain risks correlated loss. OSDs on hosts Data store What it does: The disks that hold the copies. If it fails or is weak: Unequal weights cause hot spots. Failure domains A CRUSH rule says \"choose N distinct buckets of type host\" or rack, room or datacenter. With three replicas and host failure domain, each copy is on a different server. With rack, each is in a different rack and a whole rack can fail. You need enough buckets: three racks for three copies by rack. Pools ceph osd pool create vms 128 ceph osd pool set vms size 3 ceph osd pool set vms min_size 2 ceph osd pool application enable vms rbd size is the number of copies. min_size is how many must be available to accept IO. With size 3 and min_size 2, the pool keeps working with one copy missing and stops at two missing, protecting against writing data that exists only once. Placement groups Each OSD should carry about 100 PGs. Too few makes data uneven; too many wastes memory. Leave the PG autoscaler on, and set a target_size_ratio for pools you know will grow. Incident: Two copies on one host Incident Losing a server took some data offline. $ ceph osd crush rule dump replicated_rule | grep type \"type\": \"osd\" $ ceph health detail HEALTH_ERR 12 pgs inactive; 12 pgs stuck The pool used a rule with an OSD failure domain, so two of three copies of some PGs shared a server. When that server failed, the PGs dropped below min_size. A host failure domain rule fixed the placement and data moved apart. Common mistakes Leaving the default rule without checking its failure domain Setting min_size to 1 Creating pools with a fixed PG count and never revisiting it Using a rack failure domain with fewer racks than copies"},"ceph/deploying-ceph-with-cephadm":{"html":"\u003cp>Cephadm is the standard way to deploy Ceph on bare metal or VMs. It needs a container runtime, systemd, time sync and Python, and gives you one command to bootstrap and an orchestrator for everything after.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prerequisites for a healthy first cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bootstrapping the first host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding hosts, OSDs and services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading status and finding what is wrong\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each host needs a supported Linux, systemd, a container runtime (Podman or Docker), LVM2, Python 3, time synchronisation and DNS or hosts entries. Use a separate network for cluster traffic if you can. Disks for OSDs should be empty with no partitions or file systems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bootstrap\">Bootstrap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cephadm bootstrap --mon-ip 10.0.0.11\nceph orch host add ceph2 10.0.0.12\nceph orch host add ceph3 10.0.0.13\nceph orch apply osd --all-available-devices\nceph orch ls\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">cephadm bootstrap --mon-ip 10.0.0.11\nceph orch host add ceph2 10.0.0.12\nceph orch host add ceph3 10.0.0.13\nceph orch apply osd --all-available-devices\nceph orch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Bootstrap creates the first monitor and manager, a dashboard, and an admin keyring. The orchestrator then places daemons on hosts you add.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"boot&gt;orch;spec&gt;orch;orch&gt;hosts;orch&gt;osds\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How cephadm builds a cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"boot\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cephadm bootstrap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"orch\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Orchestrator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hosts\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Added hosts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"osds\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSDs on free disks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service specification\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, cephadm bootstrap to Orchestrator; 2, Service specification to Orchestrator; 3, Orchestrator to Added hosts; 4, Orchestrator to OSDs on free disks.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"boot\">\u003cdt>cephadm bootstrap \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates the first MON and MGR and the admin keyring.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Use the right monitor IP; it is hard to change later.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"orch\">\u003cdt>Orchestrator \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs in the manager and places daemons.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the manager is down, orchestration pauses.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hosts\">\u003cdt>Added hosts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive monitors, managers and OSDs by label.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong labels place daemons in one rack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"osds\">\u003cdt>OSDs on free disks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Created from empty devices.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A disk with old data is skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spec\">\u003cdt>Service specification \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A YAML file that describes the wanted daemons.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unreviewed spec can change production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-specifications\">Service specifications\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"service_type: mon\nplacement:\n  count: 5\n  label: mon\n---\nservice_type: osd\nservice_id: hdd_with_nvme_db\nplacement:\n  host_pattern: 'osd*'\nspec:\n  data_devices:\n    rotational: 1\n  db_devices:\n    rotational: 0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service_type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">mon\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">placement:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">count:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">label:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">mon\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service_type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">osd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service_id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">hdd_with_nvme_db\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">placement:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host_pattern:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;osd*&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">data_devices:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rotational:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">db_devices:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rotational:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Apply with \u003ccode>ceph orch apply -i spec.yaml\u003c/code>. A drive group like this puts the OSD data on hard drives and their DB on flash.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-status\">Reading status\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ceph -s\u003c/code> gives health and services. \u003ccode>ceph orch ps\u003c/code> lists daemons and their state. \u003ccode>ceph health detail\u003c/code> explains each warning. \u003ccode>cephadm logs --name osd.3\u003c/code> shows a daemon&#39;s log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-disk-that-was-skipped\">Incident: The disk that was skipped\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new disk never became an OSD.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph orch device ls\nceph orch device zap osd4 /dev/sdc --force\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph orch device \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HOST   PATH      AVAILABLE  REJECT REASONS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">osd4   /dev/sdc  No         Has partitions, LVM detected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph orch device zap osd4 /dev/sdc --force\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">zap successful for /dev/sdc on osd4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The disk had an old partition table from a previous use, so cephadm refused it to protect data. The team confirmed the disk was unused, zapped it, and the orchestrator created the OSD automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping time synchronisation on hosts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running ad hoc commands that are not recorded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Zapping a disk without checking what is on it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting all monitors on the same switch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites"},{"id":"bootstrap","text":"Bootstrap"},{"id":"service-specifications","text":"Service specifications"},{"id":"reading-status","text":"Reading status"},{"id":"incident-the-disk-that-was-skipped","text":"Incident: The disk that was skipped"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"cephadm","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephadm/"},{"t":"Service specification","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephadm/services/"},{"t":"Adding hosts","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephadm/host-management/"}],"text":"Cephadm is the standard way to deploy Ceph on bare metal or VMs. It needs a container runtime, systemd, time sync and Python, and gives you one command to bootstrap and an orchestrator for everything after. What you will learn Prerequisites for a healthy first cluster Bootstrapping the first host Adding hosts, OSDs and services Reading status and finding what is wrong Prerequisites Each host needs a supported Linux, systemd, a container runtime (Podman or Docker), LVM2, Python 3, time synchronisation and DNS or hosts entries. Use a separate network for cluster traffic if you can. Disks for OSDs should be empty with no partitions or file systems. Bootstrap cephadm bootstrap --mon-ip 10.0.0.11 ceph orch host add ceph2 10.0.0.12 ceph orch host add ceph3 10.0.0.13 ceph orch apply osd --all-available-devices ceph orch ls Bootstrap creates the first monitor and manager, a dashboard, and an admin keyring. The orchestrator then places daemons on hosts you add. How cephadm builds a cluster Flow in order: 1, cephadm bootstrap to Orchestrator; 2, Service specification to Orchestrator; 3, Orchestrator to Added hosts; 4, Orchestrator to OSDs on free disks. Step Service Network edge Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. cephadm bootstrap Step What it does: Creates the first MON and MGR and the admin keyring. If it fails or is weak: Use the right monitor IP; it is hard to change later. Orchestrator Service What it does: Runs in the manager and places daemons. If it fails or is weak: If the manager is down, orchestration pauses. Added hosts Network edge What it does: Receive monitors, managers and OSDs by label. If it fails or is weak: Wrong labels place daemons in one rack. OSDs on free disks Data store What it does: Created from empty devices. If it fails or is weak: A disk with old data is skipped. Service specification Data store What it does: A YAML file that describes the wanted daemons. If it fails or is weak: An unreviewed spec can change production. Service specifications service_type: mon placement: count: 5 label: mon --- service_type: osd service_id: hdd_with_nvme_db placement: host_pattern: &#x27;osd*&#x27; spec: data_devices: rotational: 1 db_devices: rotational: 0 Apply with ceph orch apply -i spec.yaml . A drive group like this puts the OSD data on hard drives and their DB on flash. Reading status ceph -s gives health and services. ceph orch ps lists daemons and their state. ceph health detail explains each warning. cephadm logs --name osd.3 shows a daemon&#39;s log. Incident: The disk that was skipped Incident A new disk never became an OSD. $ ceph orch device ls HOST PATH AVAILABLE REJECT REASONS osd4 /dev/sdc No Has partitions, LVM detected $ ceph orch device zap osd4 /dev/sdc --force zap successful for /dev/sdc on osd4 The disk had an old partition table from a previous use, so cephadm refused it to protect data. The team confirmed the disk was unused, zapped it, and the orchestrator created the OSD automatically. Common mistakes Skipping time synchronisation on hosts Running ad hoc commands that are not recorded Zapping a disk without checking what is on it Putting all monitors on the same switch"},"ceph/rbd-block-storage-for-vms-and-kubernetes":{"html":"\u003cp>Most Ceph users meet RBD first. It gives you thin provisioned, resizable disks with snapshots and clones, spread over every OSD so no single disk limits performance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Creating and using images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Snapshots, clones and layering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using RBD with Kubernetes and VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mirroring for disaster recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"images\">Images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph osd pool create rbd 64\nrbd pool init rbd\nrbd create rbd/db1 --size 100G\nrbd info rbd/db1\nrbd map rbd/db1\nrbd du rbd/db1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph osd pool create rbd 64\nrbd pool init rbd\nrbd create rbd/db1 --size 100G\nrbd info rbd/db1\nrbd map rbd/db1\nrbd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> rbd/db1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Images are thin provisioned: they use space only as data is written. They are striped over many objects, so reads and writes hit many OSDs in parallel. Resize with \u003ccode>rbd resize\u003c/code> and grow the file system afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots-and-clones\">Snapshots and clones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A snapshot is a read-only point in time. A \u003cstrong>clone\u003c/strong> is a writable child of a protected snapshot that shares unchanged data. That makes VM provisioning fast: keep a golden image snapshot and clone it per VM. Flatten a clone to copy the data and remove the dependency on the parent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;csi;csi&gt;img;img&gt;pool;img&gt;mirror\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">RBD in a Kubernetes cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod or VM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"csi\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ceph-csi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"img\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RBD image\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replicated pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mirror\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mirror to site B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod or VM to ceph-csi; 2, ceph-csi to RBD image; 3, RBD image to Replicated pool; 4, RBD image to Mirror to site B.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod or VM \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses a PVC as a disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heavy IO from one pod can affect others.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"csi\">\u003cdt>ceph-csi \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates, maps and mounts RBD images for PVCs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong credentials leave volumes Pending.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"img\">\u003cdt>RBD image \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Thin provisioned and striped across OSDs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unremoved snapshots grow the pool.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>Replicated pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the objects with three copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A full pool stops all images in it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mirror\">\u003cdt>Mirror to site B \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copies images to another cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mirroring lag sets the data lost in a disaster.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubernetes-and-vms\">Kubernetes and VMs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A StorageClass using the \u003ccode>rbd.csi.ceph.com\u003c/code> provisioner turns a PVC into an RBD image. Use \u003ccode>volumeMode: Block\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>ReadWriteMany\u003c/code> for VMs that need live migration, and ext4 or XFS with ReadWriteOnce for databases. QEMU also talks to RBD directly through librbd.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mirroring\">Mirroring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Journal based mirroring replicates every write; snapshot based mirroring copies at intervals. Choose by how much data loss you can accept, and test a promote of the secondary site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pool-that-filled-with-snapshots\">Incident: The pool that filled with snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pool reached nearfull with little live data.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rbd du rbd/db1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>rbd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> rbd/db1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME           PROVISIONED  USED\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db1@pre-up-1   100 GiB      18 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db1@pre-up-2   100 GiB      21 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db1            100 GiB      64 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;TOTAL&gt;        100 GiB      1.9 TiB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Upgrade snapshots had accumulated for a year and each pinned changed blocks. A cleanup job that deleted snapshots older than a week returned over a terabyte of space.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keeping snapshots forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resizing the image without growing the file system\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using one pool for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping a test of the mirror promote\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"images","text":"Images"},{"id":"snapshots-and-clones","text":"Snapshots and clones"},{"id":"kubernetes-and-vms","text":"Kubernetes and VMs"},{"id":"mirroring","text":"Mirroring"},{"id":"incident-the-pool-that-filled-with-snapshots","text":"Incident: The pool that filled with snapshots"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"RBD","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rbd/"},{"t":"ceph-csi","u":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph-csi"},{"t":"RBD mirroring","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rbd/rbd-mirroring/"}],"text":"Most Ceph users meet RBD first. It gives you thin provisioned, resizable disks with snapshots and clones, spread over every OSD so no single disk limits performance. What you will learn Creating and using images Snapshots, clones and layering Using RBD with Kubernetes and VMs Mirroring for disaster recovery Images ceph osd pool create rbd 64 rbd pool init rbd rbd create rbd/db1 --size 100G rbd info rbd/db1 rbd map rbd/db1 rbd du rbd/db1 Images are thin provisioned: they use space only as data is written. They are striped over many objects, so reads and writes hit many OSDs in parallel. Resize with rbd resize and grow the file system afterwards. Snapshots and clones A snapshot is a read-only point in time. A clone is a writable child of a protected snapshot that shares unchanged data. That makes VM provisioning fast: keep a golden image snapshot and clone it per VM. Flatten a clone to copy the data and remove the dependency on the parent. RBD in a Kubernetes cluster Flow in order: 1, Pod or VM to ceph-csi; 2, ceph-csi to RBD image; 3, RBD image to Replicated pool; 4, RBD image to Mirror to site B. Service Step Data store Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod or VM Service What it does: Uses a PVC as a disk. If it fails or is weak: Heavy IO from one pod can affect others. ceph-csi Step What it does: Creates, maps and mounts RBD images for PVCs. If it fails or is weak: Wrong credentials leave volumes Pending. RBD image Data store What it does: Thin provisioned and striped across OSDs. If it fails or is weak: Unremoved snapshots grow the pool. Replicated pool Data store What it does: Holds the objects with three copies. If it fails or is weak: A full pool stops all images in it. Mirror to site B Outside party What it does: Copies images to another cluster. If it fails or is weak: Mirroring lag sets the data lost in a disaster. Kubernetes and VMs A StorageClass using the rbd.csi.ceph.com provisioner turns a PVC into an RBD image. Use volumeMode: Block with ReadWriteMany for VMs that need live migration, and ext4 or XFS with ReadWriteOnce for databases. QEMU also talks to RBD directly through librbd. Mirroring Journal based mirroring replicates every write; snapshot based mirroring copies at intervals. Choose by how much data loss you can accept, and test a promote of the secondary site. Incident: The pool that filled with snapshots Incident A pool reached nearfull with little live data. $ rbd du rbd/db1 NAME PROVISIONED USED db1@pre-up-1 100 GiB 18 GiB db1@pre-up-2 100 GiB 21 GiB db1 100 GiB 64 GiB \u003cTOTAL> 100 GiB 1.9 TiB Upgrade snapshots had accumulated for a year and each pinned changed blocks. A cleanup job that deleted snapshots older than a week returned over a terabyte of space. Common mistakes Keeping snapshots forever Resizing the image without growing the file system Using one pool for everything Skipping a test of the mirror promote"},"ceph/cephfs-shared-file-systems-and-mds":{"html":"\u003cp>CephFS fills the gap between block and object: a POSIX file system many hosts can mount at the same time. It is built on the same RADOS, with metadata servers added.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Data and metadata pools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Active and standby MDS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting, subvolumes and quotas\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using CephFS for Kubernetes ReadWriteMany\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-and-metadata\">Data and metadata\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>File contents are stored as objects in a \u003cstrong>data pool\u003c/strong>. Directory structure and file attributes live in a \u003cstrong>metadata pool\u003c/strong>, served from memory by \u003cstrong>MDS\u003c/strong> daemons. Keep the metadata pool on fast media.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph fs volume create shared\nceph fs status shared\nceph fs subvolume create shared proj-a --size 107374182400\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph fs volume create shared\nceph fs status shared\nceph fs subvolume create shared proj-a --size 107374182400\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-and-standby-mds\">Active and standby MDS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An active MDS holds part of the namespace in cache. Standby MDS daemons take over if it fails, usually in seconds. With multiple active MDS (\u003ccode>max_mds\u003c/code>), directories are split between them to spread load. The MDS cache size (\u003ccode>mds_cache_memory_limit\u003c/code>) decides how many files it can keep hot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"cli&gt;mds;mds&gt;meta;cli&gt;data;sb&gt;mds\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a CephFS client reads a file\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cli\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client mount\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mds\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Active MDS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sb\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Standby MDS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"meta\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metadata pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client mount to Active MDS; 2, Active MDS to Metadata pool; 3, Client mount to Data pool; 4, Standby MDS to Active MDS.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cli\">\u003cdt>Client mount \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A kernel or FUSE mount of the file system.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old client kernels may lack features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mds\">\u003cdt>Active MDS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Answers path, permission and layout questions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A cold or small cache makes ls slow.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sb\">\u003cdt>Standby MDS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Waits to take over a failed MDS.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow takeover stalls clients.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"meta\">\u003cdt>Metadata pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores directory and inode data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Place it on SSD or NVMe.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Data pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores file contents as objects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong replication here risks data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003cp>The client asks the MDS where a file&#39;s data is, then reads and writes the data pool directly. File data does not pass through the MDS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mounting-and-quotas\">Mounting and quotas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mount -t ceph 10.0.0.11:6789:/ /mnt/shared -o name=app,secretfile=/etc/ceph/app.secret\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">mount -t ceph 10.0.0.11:6789:/ /mnt/shared -o name=app,secretfile=/etc/ceph/app.secret\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Subvolumes isolate projects, carry quotas and can be snapshotted. Create one per tenant or application and mount only that path with its own key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubernetes\">Kubernetes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>ceph-csi provisions CephFS subvolumes as ReadWriteMany PVCs, handy for shared uploads and content. For databases, prefer RBD.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-slow-ls-in-a-huge-directory\">Incident: Slow ls in a huge directory\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Listing a directory with millions of files took minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph fs status shared\nceph tell mds.a perf dump | grep cache\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph fs status shared\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RANK  STATE   MDS    ACTIVITY     DNS   INOS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 0    active  mds.a  Reqs: 9800/s 4.1M  4.1M\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph tell mds.a perf dump | grep cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    &quot;mds_mem.ino&quot;: 4100000\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One active MDS handled every metadata request and had a cache too small for four million files. The team raised max_mds, increased the cache limit, and changed the application to spread files over subdirectories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting the metadata pool on slow disks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using CephFS for databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One huge shared directory for all users\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting the whole file system for every application\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"data-and-metadata","text":"Data and metadata"},{"id":"active-and-standby-mds","text":"Active and standby MDS"},{"id":"mounting-and-quotas","text":"Mounting and quotas"},{"id":"kubernetes","text":"Kubernetes"},{"id":"incident-slow-ls-in-a-huge-directory","text":"Incident: Slow ls in a huge directory"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CephFS","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephfs/"},{"t":"CephFS subvolumes","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephfs/fs-volumes/"},{"t":"CephFS and CSI","u":"https://github.com/ceph/ceph-csi"}],"text":"CephFS fills the gap between block and object: a POSIX file system many hosts can mount at the same time. It is built on the same RADOS, with metadata servers added. What you will learn Data and metadata pools Active and standby MDS Mounting, subvolumes and quotas Using CephFS for Kubernetes ReadWriteMany Data and metadata File contents are stored as objects in a data pool . Directory structure and file attributes live in a metadata pool , served from memory by MDS daemons. Keep the metadata pool on fast media. ceph fs volume create shared ceph fs status shared ceph fs subvolume create shared proj-a --size 107374182400 Active and standby MDS An active MDS holds part of the namespace in cache. Standby MDS daemons take over if it fails, usually in seconds. With multiple active MDS ( max_mds ), directories are split between them to spread load. The MDS cache size ( mds_cache_memory_limit ) decides how many files it can keep hot. How a CephFS client reads a file Flow in order: 1, Client mount to Active MDS; 2, Active MDS to Metadata pool; 3, Client mount to Data pool; 4, Standby MDS to Active MDS. Client Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client mount Client What it does: A kernel or FUSE mount of the file system. If it fails or is weak: Old client kernels may lack features. Active MDS Service What it does: Answers path, permission and layout questions. If it fails or is weak: A cold or small cache makes ls slow. Standby MDS Service What it does: Waits to take over a failed MDS. If it fails or is weak: A slow takeover stalls clients. Metadata pool Data store What it does: Stores directory and inode data. If it fails or is weak: Place it on SSD or NVMe. Data pool Data store What it does: Stores file contents as objects. If it fails or is weak: Wrong replication here risks data. The client asks the MDS where a file&#39;s data is, then reads and writes the data pool directly. File data does not pass through the MDS. Mounting and quotas mount -t ceph 10.0.0.11:6789:/ /mnt/shared -o name=app,secretfile=/etc/ceph/app.secret Subvolumes isolate projects, carry quotas and can be snapshotted. Create one per tenant or application and mount only that path with its own key. Kubernetes ceph-csi provisions CephFS subvolumes as ReadWriteMany PVCs, handy for shared uploads and content. For databases, prefer RBD. Incident: Slow ls in a huge directory Incident Listing a directory with millions of files took minutes. $ ceph fs status shared RANK STATE MDS ACTIVITY DNS INOS 0 active mds.a Reqs: 9800/s 4.1M 4.1M $ ceph tell mds.a perf dump | grep cache \"mds_mem.ino\": 4100000 One active MDS handled every metadata request and had a cache too small for four million files. The team raised max_mds, increased the cache limit, and changed the application to spread files over subdirectories. Common mistakes Putting the metadata pool on slow disks Using CephFS for databases One huge shared directory for all users Mounting the whole file system for every application"},"ceph/rgw-s3-compatible-object-storage-with-ceph":{"html":"\u003cp>With RGW you can offer an S3 compatible service from your own data centre. It is common for backups, artifacts, data lakes and applications that must keep data on site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How RGW stores objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Users, keys, buckets and policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Index sharding and lifecycle\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Multisite replication\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>RGW is a stateless HTTP gateway. It translates S3 requests into RADOS operations on several pools: one for object data, others for bucket indexes, logs and metadata. Run at least two RGW daemons behind a load balancer such as HAProxy and terminate TLS there or in RGW.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"radosgw-admin user create --uid=backup --display-name=&quot;Backup app&quot;\naws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 mb s3://nightly\naws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 cp db.dump s3://nightly/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">radosgw-admin user create --uid=backup --display-name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Backup app&quot;\u003c/span>\naws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 mb s3://nightly\naws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> db.dump s3://nightly/\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"users-and-policies\">Users and policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each application gets its own user, access key and secret key, with quotas. Bucket policies and IAM style policies control access. Never share the admin user&#39;s keys with applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;lb;lb&gt;rgw;rgw&gt;idx;rgw&gt;obj\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">S3 requests through RGW\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">S3 client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer + TLS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rgw\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">RGW daemons\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"idx\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bucket index pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obj\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data pool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, S3 client to Load balancer + TLS; 2, Load balancer + TLS to RGW daemons; 3, RGW daemons to Bucket index pool; 4, RGW daemons to Data pool.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>S3 client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs requests with an access key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keys in code leak through repositories.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer + TLS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Spreads requests and ends HTTPS.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A single balancer is a single point of failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rgw\">\u003cdt>RGW daemons \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Translate S3 into RADOS operations.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too few daemons limit throughput.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"idx\">\u003cdt>Bucket index pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Maps object names to locations.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Huge buckets need index shards.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obj\">\u003cdt>Data pool \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds object data, often erasure coded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Small objects waste space on EC.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"index-sharding-and-lifecycle\">Index sharding and lifecycle\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Listing a bucket reads its index. A bucket with millions of objects needs several index shards, and dynamic resharding adds them automatically. Lifecycle rules expire or transition objects by age, which keeps temporary data from filling the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multisite\">Multisite\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>realm\u003c/strong> contains \u003cstrong>zone groups\u003c/strong>, which contain \u003cstrong>zones\u003c/strong>. Each zone is its own cluster. Metadata and data replicate between zones asynchronously, so a second site holds a copy for disaster recovery or local reads. Replication is eventually consistent, so applications must tolerate a short lag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bucket-too-big-to-list\">Incident: A bucket too big to list\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Listing a bucket timed out after months of backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"radosgw-admin bucket limit check\nradosgw-admin bucket reshard add --bucket=nightly --num-shards=211\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>radosgw-admin bucket \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">limit\u003c/span> check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;bucket&quot;: &quot;nightly&quot;, &quot;num_objects&quot;: 410000000, &quot;num_shards&quot;: 11, &quot;fill_status&quot;: &quot;OVER 100%&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>radosgw-admin bucket reshard add --bucket=nightly --num-shards=211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">queued for resharding\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The bucket index had far too few shards for the number of objects, so listing and writes slowed. Resharding it to more shards, and turning on dynamic resharding, restored normal speed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sharing the admin credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One RGW daemon with no load balancer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never setting lifecycle rules on temporary buckets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting multisite to be instant\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works"},{"id":"users-and-policies","text":"Users and policies"},{"id":"index-sharding-and-lifecycle","text":"Index sharding and lifecycle"},{"id":"multisite","text":"Multisite"},{"id":"incident-a-bucket-too-big-to-list","text":"Incident: A bucket too big to list"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ceph Object Gateway","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/radosgw/"},{"t":"RGW multisite","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/radosgw/multisite/"},{"t":"S3 API support","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/radosgw/s3/"}],"text":"With RGW you can offer an S3 compatible service from your own data centre. It is common for backups, artifacts, data lakes and applications that must keep data on site. What you will learn How RGW stores objects Users, keys, buckets and policies Index sharding and lifecycle Multisite replication How it works RGW is a stateless HTTP gateway. It translates S3 requests into RADOS operations on several pools: one for object data, others for bucket indexes, logs and metadata. Run at least two RGW daemons behind a load balancer such as HAProxy and terminate TLS there or in RGW. radosgw-admin user create --uid=backup --display-name= \"Backup app\" aws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 mb s3://nightly aws --endpoint-url https://s3.example.com s3 cp db.dump s3://nightly/ Users and policies Each application gets its own user, access key and secret key, with quotas. Bucket policies and IAM style policies control access. Never share the admin user&#39;s keys with applications. S3 requests through RGW Flow in order: 1, S3 client to Load balancer + TLS; 2, Load balancer + TLS to RGW daemons; 3, RGW daemons to Bucket index pool; 4, RGW daemons to Data pool. Client Network edge Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. S3 client Client What it does: Signs requests with an access key. If it fails or is weak: Keys in code leak through repositories. Load balancer + TLS Network edge What it does: Spreads requests and ends HTTPS. If it fails or is weak: A single balancer is a single point of failure. RGW daemons Service What it does: Translate S3 into RADOS operations. If it fails or is weak: Too few daemons limit throughput. Bucket index pool Data store What it does: Maps object names to locations. If it fails or is weak: Huge buckets need index shards. Data pool Data store What it does: Holds object data, often erasure coded. If it fails or is weak: Small objects waste space on EC. Index sharding and lifecycle Listing a bucket reads its index. A bucket with millions of objects needs several index shards, and dynamic resharding adds them automatically. Lifecycle rules expire or transition objects by age, which keeps temporary data from filling the cluster. Multisite A realm contains zone groups , which contain zones . Each zone is its own cluster. Metadata and data replicate between zones asynchronously, so a second site holds a copy for disaster recovery or local reads. Replication is eventually consistent, so applications must tolerate a short lag. Incident: A bucket too big to list Incident Listing a bucket timed out after months of backups. $ radosgw-admin bucket limit check \"bucket\": \"nightly\", \"num_objects\": 410000000, \"num_shards\": 11, \"fill_status\": \"OVER 100%\" $ radosgw-admin bucket reshard add --bucket=nightly --num-shards=211 queued for resharding The bucket index had far too few shards for the number of objects, so listing and writes slowed. Resharding it to more shards, and turning on dynamic resharding, restored normal speed. Common mistakes Sharing the admin credentials One RGW daemon with no load balancer Never setting lifecycle rules on temporary buckets Expecting multisite to be instant"},"ceph/rook-ceph-on-kubernetes":{"html":"\u003cp>Running Ceph inside Kubernetes is attractive: one platform, declarative storage, and the operator replaces daemons that fail. It also couples your storage lifecycle to cluster operations, which needs care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the Rook operator manages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining the cluster and its pools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hyperconverged and dedicated storage nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Day two: toolbox, upgrades and safe node maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-operator\">The operator\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rook runs as a Deployment. You create a \u003ccode>CephCluster\u003c/code> custom resource and it starts monitors, managers and OSD pods, one OSD per device. If a pod dies, Kubernetes and the operator restart it. Rook also installs ceph-csi so PVCs can use the pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: ceph.rook.io/v1\nkind: CephCluster\nmetadata:\n  name: rook-ceph\n  namespace: rook-ceph\nspec:\n  cephVersion:\n    image: quay.io/ceph/ceph:v18\n  dataDirHostPath: /var/lib/rook\n  mon:\n    count: 3\n    allowMultiplePerNode: false\n  storage:\n    useAllNodes: false\n    useAllDevices: false\n    nodes:\n      - name: storage1\n        devices:\n          - name: nvme1n1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ceph.rook.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CephCluster\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rook-ceph\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rook-ceph\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cephVersion:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">quay.io/ceph/ceph:v18\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dataDirHostPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/var/lib/rook\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mon:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">count:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowMultiplePerNode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">useAllNodes:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">useAllDevices:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nodes:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">storage1\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">devices:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nvme1n1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pools-and-classes\">Pools and classes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: ceph.rook.io/v1\nkind: CephBlockPool\nmetadata:\n  name: replicapool\n  namespace: rook-ceph\nspec:\n  failureDomain: host\n  replicated:\n    size: 3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ceph.rook.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CephBlockPool\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">replicapool\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rook-ceph\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureDomain:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">host\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicated:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">size:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A StorageClass then points at this pool. PVCs from it become RBD images.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"cr&gt;op;op&gt;pods;op&gt;sc;sc&gt;app\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Rook turning a custom resource into storage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cr\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CephCluster resource\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"op\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rook operator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pods\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">MON, MGR and OSD pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">StorageClass + CSI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application PVC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, CephCluster resource to Rook operator; 2, Rook operator to MON, MGR and OSD pods; 3, Rook operator to StorageClass + CSI; 4, StorageClass + CSI to Application PVC.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cr\">\u003cdt>CephCluster resource \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Declares monitors, devices and the Ceph version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong device list can wipe a disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"op\">\u003cdt>Rook operator \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates and heals the Ceph pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the operator is down nothing is repaired.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pods\">\u003cdt>MON, MGR and OSD pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The running Ceph daemons.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pods on one node share a failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sc\">\u003cdt>StorageClass + CSI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Turns PVCs into RBD or CephFS volumes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong pool names leave PVCs Pending.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application PVC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses the storage like any volume.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heavy writers need their own pool or class.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-run-it\">Where to run it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hyperconverged means storage and applications share nodes, which saves hardware but couples CPU and memory use. Dedicated storage nodes, selected with labels and taints, give steadier performance. Either way, OSD pods need local disks or PVCs from a fast class.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"day-two\">Day two\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>toolbox\u003c/strong> pod gives you the \u003ccode>ceph\u003c/code> command: \u003ccode>kubectl -n rook-ceph exec -it deploy/rook-ceph-tools -- ceph -s\u003c/code>. Upgrade the operator first, then the Ceph image. Before draining a node, check that the cluster is healthy and that removing the node&#39;s OSDs keeps enough replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-drain-that-took-data-offline\">Incident: The drain that took data offline\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node drain started a cascade of warnings.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl -n rook-ceph exec deploy/rook-ceph-tools -- ceph -s\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n rook-ceph \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/rook-ceph-tools -- ceph -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    health: HEALTH_WARN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">            Degraded data redundancy: 412/9120 objects degraded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">storage2   Ready,SchedulingDisabled\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A second node was drained before recovery from the first finished, so some data had one copy. The team now waits for HEALTH_OK between drains and uses Pod Disruption Budgets that Rook creates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Draining a second storage node before the cluster is healthy again\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using useAllDevices on nodes with data disks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing storage and heavy application load without limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading Ceph before the operator\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-operator","text":"The operator"},{"id":"pools-and-classes","text":"Pools and classes"},{"id":"where-to-run-it","text":"Where to run it"},{"id":"day-two","text":"Day two"},{"id":"incident-the-drain-that-took-data-offline","text":"Incident: The drain that took data offline"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Rook documentation","u":"https://rook.io/docs/rook/latest-release/"},{"t":"Rook Ceph storage","u":"https://rook.io/docs/rook/latest-release/Storage-Configuration/ceph-teardown/"},{"t":"Kubernetes storage classes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/"}],"text":"Running Ceph inside Kubernetes is attractive: one platform, declarative storage, and the operator replaces daemons that fail. It also couples your storage lifecycle to cluster operations, which needs care. What you will learn What the Rook operator manages Defining the cluster and its pools Hyperconverged and dedicated storage nodes Day two: toolbox, upgrades and safe node maintenance The operator Rook runs as a Deployment. You create a CephCluster custom resource and it starts monitors, managers and OSD pods, one OSD per device. If a pod dies, Kubernetes and the operator restart it. Rook also installs ceph-csi so PVCs can use the pools. apiVersion: ceph.rook.io/v1 kind: CephCluster metadata: name: rook-ceph namespace: rook-ceph spec: cephVersion: image: quay.io/ceph/ceph:v18 dataDirHostPath: /var/lib/rook mon: count: 3 allowMultiplePerNode: false storage: useAllNodes: false useAllDevices: false nodes: - name: storage1 devices: - name: nvme1n1 Pools and classes apiVersion: ceph.rook.io/v1 kind: CephBlockPool metadata: name: replicapool namespace: rook-ceph spec: failureDomain: host replicated: size: 3 A StorageClass then points at this pool. PVCs from it become RBD images. Rook turning a custom resource into storage Flow in order: 1, CephCluster resource to Rook operator; 2, Rook operator to MON, MGR and OSD pods; 3, Rook operator to StorageClass + CSI; 4, StorageClass + CSI to Application PVC. Data store Step Service Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. CephCluster resource Data store What it does: Declares monitors, devices and the Ceph version. If it fails or is weak: A wrong device list can wipe a disk. Rook operator Step What it does: Creates and heals the Ceph pods. If it fails or is weak: If the operator is down nothing is repaired. MON, MGR and OSD pods Service What it does: The running Ceph daemons. If it fails or is weak: Pods on one node share a failure. StorageClass + CSI Step What it does: Turns PVCs into RBD or CephFS volumes. If it fails or is weak: Wrong pool names leave PVCs Pending. Application PVC Client What it does: Uses the storage like any volume. If it fails or is weak: Heavy writers need their own pool or class. Where to run it Hyperconverged means storage and applications share nodes, which saves hardware but couples CPU and memory use. Dedicated storage nodes, selected with labels and taints, give steadier performance. Either way, OSD pods need local disks or PVCs from a fast class. Day two The toolbox pod gives you the ceph command: kubectl -n rook-ceph exec -it deploy/rook-ceph-tools -- ceph -s . Upgrade the operator first, then the Ceph image. Before draining a node, check that the cluster is healthy and that removing the node&#39;s OSDs keeps enough replicas. Incident: The drain that took data offline Incident A node drain started a cascade of warnings. $ kubectl -n rook-ceph exec deploy/rook-ceph-tools -- ceph -s health: HEALTH_WARN Degraded data redundancy: 412/9120 objects degraded $ kubectl get nodes storage2 Ready,SchedulingDisabled A second node was drained before recovery from the first finished, so some data had one copy. The team now waits for HEALTH_OK between drains and uses Pod Disruption Budgets that Rook creates. Common mistakes Draining a second storage node before the cluster is healthy again Using useAllDevices on nodes with data disks Mixing storage and heavy application load without limits Upgrading Ceph before the operator"},"ceph/replication-erasure-coding-and-durability":{"html":"\u003cp>Capacity and durability pull in different directions. Ceph lets you pick per pool, so you can match the protection to what the data is worth and how it is accessed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replicated pools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Erasure coding with k and m\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity overhead and tolerance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a layout for the workload\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication\">Replication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Replicated pools keep \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> full copies on different failure domains. They are simple and fast, repair quickly by copying one replica, and handle small random writes well. The cost is raw space: three copies use three times the data size.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"erasure-coding\">Erasure coding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An EC profile splits each object into \u003cstrong>k\u003c/strong> data chunks and \u003cstrong>m\u003c/strong> coding chunks placed on different failure domains. Any k of the k+m chunks can rebuild the object, so the pool survives m failures.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Layout\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Raw space per usable\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Survives\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Replicated size 3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3.0x\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 lost copies\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast, simple\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>EC 4+2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.5x\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2 lost chunks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Needs at least 6 hosts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>EC 8+3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.375x\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3 lost chunks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Needs at least 11 hosts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph osd erasure-code-profile set ec42 k=4 m=2 crush-failure-domain=host\nceph osd pool create archive 64 64 erasure ec42\nceph osd pool set archive allow_ec_overwrites true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph osd erasure-code-profile \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> ec42 k=4 m=2 crush-failure-domain=host\nceph osd pool create archive 64 64 erasure ec42\nceph osd pool \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> archive allow_ec_overwrites \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"data&gt;rep;data&gt;ec;rep&gt;fail;ec&gt;fail\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Three copies versus four plus two\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">12 TB of data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rep\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replicated size 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Erasure coded 4+2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fail\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Two failures\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, 12 TB of data to Replicated size 3; 2, 12 TB of data to Erasure coded 4+2; 3, Replicated size 3 to Two failures; 4, Erasure coded 4+2 to Two failures.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>12 TB of data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The amount users store.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plan raw capacity from this number.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rep\">\u003cdt>Replicated size 3 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> 36 TB raw, fast and simple.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expensive for large cold data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ec\">\u003cdt>Erasure coded 4+2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> 18 TB raw with two parity chunks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slower small writes and heavier recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fail\">\u003cdt>Two failures \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Both layouts survive losing two failure domains.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A third loss in either layout means data loss.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use replicated pools for RBD databases, VM disks and CephFS metadata. Use erasure coding for RGW data, backups and archives, where objects are large and written once. Block devices on EC need \u003ccode>allow_ec_overwrites\u003c/code> and a replicated pool for metadata, and still suit sequential rather than random IO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"durability-is-more-than-copies\">Durability is more than copies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Durability also depends on failure domains, how fast recovery runs, scrubbing that finds bit rot, and spare capacity to rebuild into. A cluster 95 percent full cannot recover from a failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-not-enough-hosts-for-the-profile\">Incident: Not enough hosts for the profile\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Creating an EC pool left PGs stuck.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph health detail\nceph osd tree | grep -c host\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health detail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_WARN Reduced data availability: 64 pgs inactive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pg 7.3 is creating+incomplete, acting [4,9,NONE,14,2,NONE]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph osd tree | grep -c host\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A 4+2 profile with host failure domain needs six hosts. With five hosts, two chunks of each PG had nowhere to go. The team added a sixth host, and the PGs became active.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using EC for small random write workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing k and m with fewer hosts than k+m\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting raw space instead of usable space\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running above 80 percent full and expecting to recover\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"replication","text":"Replication"},{"id":"erasure-coding","text":"Erasure coding"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"durability-is-more-than-copies","text":"Durability is more than copies"},{"id":"incident-not-enough-hosts-for-the-profile","text":"Incident: Not enough hosts for the profile"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Erasure coding","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/erasure-code/"},{"t":"Pools","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/pools/"},{"t":"Data durability and recovery","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/architecture/"}],"text":"Capacity and durability pull in different directions. Ceph lets you pick per pool, so you can match the protection to what the data is worth and how it is accessed. What you will learn Replicated pools Erasure coding with k and m Capacity overhead and tolerance Choosing a layout for the workload Replication Replicated pools keep size full copies on different failure domains. They are simple and fast, repair quickly by copying one replica, and handle small random writes well. The cost is raw space: three copies use three times the data size. Erasure coding An EC profile splits each object into k data chunks and m coding chunks placed on different failure domains. Any k of the k+m chunks can rebuild the object, so the pool survives m failures. Layout Raw space per usable Survives Notes Replicated size 3 3.0x 2 lost copies Fast, simple EC 4+2 1.5x 2 lost chunks Needs at least 6 hosts EC 8+3 1.375x 3 lost chunks Needs at least 11 hosts ceph osd erasure-code-profile set ec42 k=4 m=2 crush-failure-domain=host ceph osd pool create archive 64 64 erasure ec42 ceph osd pool set archive allow_ec_overwrites true Three copies versus four plus two Flow in order: 1, 12 TB of data to Replicated size 3; 2, 12 TB of data to Erasure coded 4+2; 3, Replicated size 3 to Two failures; 4, Erasure coded 4+2 to Two failures. Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. 12 TB of data Data store What it does: The amount users store. If it fails or is weak: Plan raw capacity from this number. Replicated size 3 Data store What it does: 36 TB raw, fast and simple. If it fails or is weak: Expensive for large cold data. Erasure coded 4+2 Data store What it does: 18 TB raw with two parity chunks. If it fails or is weak: Slower small writes and heavier recovery. Two failures Observability What it does: Both layouts survive losing two failure domains. If it fails or is weak: A third loss in either layout means data loss. Choosing Use replicated pools for RBD databases, VM disks and CephFS metadata. Use erasure coding for RGW data, backups and archives, where objects are large and written once. Block devices on EC need allow_ec_overwrites and a replicated pool for metadata, and still suit sequential rather than random IO. Durability is more than copies Durability also depends on failure domains, how fast recovery runs, scrubbing that finds bit rot, and spare capacity to rebuild into. A cluster 95 percent full cannot recover from a failure. Incident: Not enough hosts for the profile Incident Creating an EC pool left PGs stuck. $ ceph health detail HEALTH_WARN Reduced data availability: 64 pgs inactive pg 7.3 is creating+incomplete, acting [4,9,NONE,14,2,NONE] $ ceph osd tree | grep -c host 5 A 4+2 profile with host failure domain needs six hosts. With five hosts, two chunks of each PG had nowhere to go. The team added a sixth host, and the PGs became active. Common mistakes Using EC for small random write workloads Choosing k and m with fewer hosts than k+m Counting raw space instead of usable space Running above 80 percent full and expecting to recover"},"ceph/bluestore-networks-and-ceph-performance":{"html":"\u003cp>A Ceph write travels from the client to a primary OSD, then to its replicas over the network, and is acknowledged only when all have it. Anything slow along that path slows the write.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing media and where the DB and WAL go\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Public and cluster networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Benchmarking before and after a change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping recovery from hurting clients\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"media\">Media\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>NVMe gives the best latency for RBD databases. SSD suits general VM storage. Hard drives suit capacity for object data, with DB and WAL on flash. Use enterprise drives with power loss protection, because Ceph asks drives to flush data safely. Plan memory: the default \u003ccode>osd_memory_target\u003c/code> is 4 GiB per OSD.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"networks\">Networks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Client traffic uses the \u003cstrong>public network\u003c/strong>. Replication, recovery and heartbeats can use a separate \u003cstrong>cluster network\u003c/strong>. Because each client write becomes two further replica writes, cluster traffic is roughly twice the client load, plus recovery. Use at least 25 GbE for dense nodes, and make sure MTU matches on every switch if you use jumbo frames.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"cli&gt;pri;pri&gt;r1;pri&gt;r2;r1&gt;ack;r2&gt;ack\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A replicated write\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cli\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pri\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Primary OSD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"r1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replica OSD 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"r2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replica OSD 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ack\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Acknowledge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Primary OSD; 2, Primary OSD to Replica OSD 2; 3, Primary OSD to Replica OSD 3; 4, Replica OSD 2 to Acknowledge; 5, Replica OSD 3 to Acknowledge.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cli\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends the write to the primary OSD.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow client link caps throughput.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pri\">\u003cdt>Primary OSD \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes locally and forwards to replicas.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One slow disk delays every write that uses it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"r1\">\u003cdt>Replica OSD 2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes the copy and acknowledges.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cluster network congestion slows it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"r2\">\u003cdt>Replica OSD 3 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes the copy and acknowledges.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A busy recovery competes for the same disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ack\">\u003cdt>Acknowledge \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sent to the client after all copies are safe.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Latency is the slowest replica.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"benchmarks\">Benchmarks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rados bench -p testpool 30 write --no-cleanup\nrados bench -p testpool 30 seq\nrbd bench --io-type write --io-size 4K --io-threads 16 rbd/test\nfio --name=t --filename=/dev/rbd0 --rw=randwrite --bs=4k --iodepth=32 --runtime=60 --direct=1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">rados bench -p testpool 30 write --no-cleanup\nrados bench -p testpool 30 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">seq\u003c/span>\nrbd bench --io-type write --io-size 4K --io-threads 16 rbd/test\nfio --name=t --filename=/dev/rbd0 --rw=randwrite --bs=4k --iodepth=32 --runtime=60 --direct=1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run the same test before and after each change, and run it from more than one client. A single client may hit its own limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery-and-scrub\">Recovery and scrub\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Recovery and backfill compete with clients. The \u003cstrong>mClock\u003c/strong> scheduler lets you choose a profile such as \u003ccode>high_client_ops\u003c/code>. Schedule scrubs outside business hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-latency-spikes-after-every-disk-swap\">Incident: Latency spikes after every disk swap\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Replacing a failed disk made VMs slow for hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s | grep -E 'recovery|client'\nceph config get osd osd_mclock_profile\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph -s | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;recovery|client&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    client:   220 MiB/s rd, 90 MiB/s wr\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    recovery: 780 MiB/s, 190 objects/s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph config get osd osd_mclock_profile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">high_recovery_ops\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster had been left on the high_recovery_ops profile after an earlier rebuild, so recovery used most of the disk bandwidth. Switching to balanced kept clients responsive while data healed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using consumer SSDs without power loss protection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several settings at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Benchmarking from one client only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving recovery at maximum priority\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"media","text":"Media"},{"id":"networks","text":"Networks"},{"id":"benchmarks","text":"Benchmarks"},{"id":"recovery-and-scrub","text":"Recovery and scrub"},{"id":"incident-latency-spikes-after-every-disk-swap","text":"Incident: Latency spikes after every disk swap"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Hardware recommendations","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/start/hardware-recommendations/"},{"t":"Benchmarking","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/control/"},{"t":"Network configuration","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/configuration/network-config-ref/"}],"text":"A Ceph write travels from the client to a primary OSD, then to its replicas over the network, and is acknowledged only when all have it. Anything slow along that path slows the write. What you will learn Choosing media and where the DB and WAL go Public and cluster networks Benchmarking before and after a change Keeping recovery from hurting clients Media NVMe gives the best latency for RBD databases. SSD suits general VM storage. Hard drives suit capacity for object data, with DB and WAL on flash. Use enterprise drives with power loss protection, because Ceph asks drives to flush data safely. Plan memory: the default osd_memory_target is 4 GiB per OSD. Networks Client traffic uses the public network . Replication, recovery and heartbeats can use a separate cluster network . Because each client write becomes two further replica writes, cluster traffic is roughly twice the client load, plus recovery. Use at least 25 GbE for dense nodes, and make sure MTU matches on every switch if you use jumbo frames. A replicated write Flow in order: 1, Client to Primary OSD; 2, Primary OSD to Replica OSD 2; 3, Primary OSD to Replica OSD 3; 4, Replica OSD 2 to Acknowledge; 5, Replica OSD 3 to Acknowledge. Client Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Client What it does: Sends the write to the primary OSD. If it fails or is weak: A slow client link caps throughput. Primary OSD Data store What it does: Writes locally and forwards to replicas. If it fails or is weak: One slow disk delays every write that uses it. Replica OSD 2 Data store What it does: Writes the copy and acknowledges. If it fails or is weak: Cluster network congestion slows it. Replica OSD 3 Data store What it does: Writes the copy and acknowledges. If it fails or is weak: A busy recovery competes for the same disk. Acknowledge Step What it does: Sent to the client after all copies are safe. If it fails or is weak: Latency is the slowest replica. Benchmarks rados bench -p testpool 30 write --no-cleanup rados bench -p testpool 30 seq rbd bench --io-type write --io-size 4K --io-threads 16 rbd/test fio --name=t --filename=/dev/rbd0 --rw=randwrite --bs=4k --iodepth=32 --runtime=60 --direct=1 Run the same test before and after each change, and run it from more than one client. A single client may hit its own limit. Recovery and scrub Recovery and backfill compete with clients. The mClock scheduler lets you choose a profile such as high_client_ops . Schedule scrubs outside business hours. Incident: Latency spikes after every disk swap Incident Replacing a failed disk made VMs slow for hours. $ ceph -s | grep -E &#x27;recovery|client&#x27; client: 220 MiB/s rd, 90 MiB/s wr recovery: 780 MiB/s, 190 objects/s $ ceph config get osd osd_mclock_profile high_recovery_ops The cluster had been left on the high_recovery_ops profile after an earlier rebuild, so recovery used most of the disk bandwidth. Switching to balanced kept clients responsive while data healed. Common mistakes Using consumer SSDs without power loss protection Changing several settings at once Benchmarking from one client only Leaving recovery at maximum priority"},"ceph/ceph-failures-recovery-and-health-warnings":{"html":"\u003cp>In Ceph, status is information. Most warnings are the cluster telling you it is healing. A few mean data is at risk or writes are about to stop. Learn to tell them apart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading ceph -s and health detail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disk and host failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planned maintenance with noout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity thresholds, slow ops and inconsistent PGs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-look\">Where to look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph -s\nceph health detail\nceph osd tree\nceph pg stat\nceph osd df\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph -s\nceph health detail\nceph osd tree\nceph pg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>\nceph osd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ceph -s\u003c/code> shows health, daemons and PG states. \u003ccode>ceph health detail\u003c/code> explains each warning with the affected daemons. Healthy PGs are \u003ccode>active+clean\u003c/code>. States such as \u003ccode>degraded\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>undersized\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>backfilling\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>remapped\u003c/code> show work in progress. \u003ccode>inactive\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>incomplete\u003c/code> means IO is blocked and is urgent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failures\">Failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When an OSD stops, its PGs run with fewer copies (degraded) but keep serving IO as long as min_size is met. After the down-to-out timeout, Ceph rebuilds the missing copies on other OSDs. If you replace a failed disk, remove the old OSD and create a new one; data flows back by backfill.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"fail&gt;out;out&gt;back;fail&gt;swap;swap&gt;back;back&gt;ok\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Handling a failed disk\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fail\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">OSD goes down\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Marked out after timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"swap\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replace the disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backfill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ok\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">active+clean\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, OSD goes down to Marked out after timeout; 2, Marked out after timeout to Backfill; 3, OSD goes down to Replace the disk; 4, Replace the disk to Backfill; 5, Backfill to active+clean.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fail\">\u003cdt>OSD goes down \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Its PGs are degraded but still served.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Two failures in the same PG are the danger.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Marked out after timeout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The cluster starts rebuilding copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Recovery load can slow clients.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"swap\">\u003cdt>Replace the disk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Remove the old OSD and add a new one.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zap the right disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Backfill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Data flows to the new OSD.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Watch the progress in ceph -s.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ok\">\u003cdt>active+clean \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> All PGs have full copies again.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Only then move to the next change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"planned-maintenance\">Planned maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>ceph osd set noout\u003c/code> before rebooting a host so its OSDs are not marked out and rebuilt unnecessarily. Reboot one host, wait for the OSDs to rejoin and PGs to return to clean, then \u003ccode>ceph osd unset noout\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-warnings\">Common warnings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Warning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>nearfull (85%)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An OSD is almost full\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add capacity or delete data now\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>backfillfull (90%)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backfill to that OSD stops\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Free space before more failures\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>full (95%)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Writes are blocked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Emergency: add space or delete\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>slow ops\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Requests wait on slow OSDs or network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Check disks and network\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>clock skew\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Monitors disagree on time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fix NTP or chrony\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>inconsistent PG\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Scrub found a mismatch\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Investigate, then \u003ccode>ceph pg repair\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-reboots-in-a-row\">Incident: Three reboots in a row\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rebooting hosts one after another made data unavailable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph pg stat\nceph health detail | head -n 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph pg \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2048 pgs: 188 active+undersized+degraded, 31 down, 1829 active+clean\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health detail | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -n 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_ERR Reduced data availability: 31 pgs inactive, 31 pgs down\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The operator rebooted the second and third hosts before recovery from the first had finished. Some PGs then had only one copy, below min_size. Bringing a host back restored IO. The team wrote a rule: set noout, one host at a time, wait for HEALTH_OK.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebooting several storage hosts at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring nearfull until the cluster is full\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running repair before understanding why a PG is inconsistent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to unset noout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-to-look","text":"Where to look"},{"id":"failures","text":"Failures"},{"id":"planned-maintenance","text":"Planned maintenance"},{"id":"common-warnings","text":"Common warnings"},{"id":"incident-three-reboots-in-a-row","text":"Incident: Three reboots in a row"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Troubleshooting OSDs","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-osd/"},{"t":"Troubleshooting PGs","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-pg/"},{"t":"Health checks","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/health-checks/"}],"text":"In Ceph, status is information. Most warnings are the cluster telling you it is healing. A few mean data is at risk or writes are about to stop. Learn to tell them apart. What you will learn Reading ceph -s and health detail Disk and host failures Planned maintenance with noout Capacity thresholds, slow ops and inconsistent PGs Where to look ceph -s ceph health detail ceph osd tree ceph pg stat ceph osd df ceph -s shows health, daemons and PG states. ceph health detail explains each warning with the affected daemons. Healthy PGs are active+clean . States such as degraded , undersized , backfilling and remapped show work in progress. inactive or incomplete means IO is blocked and is urgent. Failures When an OSD stops, its PGs run with fewer copies (degraded) but keep serving IO as long as min_size is met. After the down-to-out timeout, Ceph rebuilds the missing copies on other OSDs. If you replace a failed disk, remove the old OSD and create a new one; data flows back by backfill. Handling a failed disk Flow in order: 1, OSD goes down to Marked out after timeout; 2, Marked out after timeout to Backfill; 3, OSD goes down to Replace the disk; 4, Replace the disk to Backfill; 5, Backfill to active+clean. Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. OSD goes down Observability What it does: Its PGs are degraded but still served. If it fails or is weak: Two failures in the same PG are the danger. Marked out after timeout Step What it does: The cluster starts rebuilding copies. If it fails or is weak: Recovery load can slow clients. Replace the disk Step What it does: Remove the old OSD and add a new one. If it fails or is weak: Zap the right disk. Backfill Step What it does: Data flows to the new OSD. If it fails or is weak: Watch the progress in ceph -s. active+clean Data store What it does: All PGs have full copies again. If it fails or is weak: Only then move to the next change. Planned maintenance Run ceph osd set noout before rebooting a host so its OSDs are not marked out and rebuilt unnecessarily. Reboot one host, wait for the OSDs to rejoin and PGs to return to clean, then ceph osd unset noout . Common warnings Warning Meaning Action nearfull (85%) An OSD is almost full Add capacity or delete data now backfillfull (90%) Backfill to that OSD stops Free space before more failures full (95%) Writes are blocked Emergency: add space or delete slow ops Requests wait on slow OSDs or network Check disks and network clock skew Monitors disagree on time Fix NTP or chrony inconsistent PG Scrub found a mismatch Investigate, then ceph pg repair Incident: Three reboots in a row Incident Rebooting hosts one after another made data unavailable. $ ceph pg stat 2048 pgs: 188 active+undersized+degraded, 31 down, 1829 active+clean $ ceph health detail | head -n 3 HEALTH_ERR Reduced data availability: 31 pgs inactive, 31 pgs down The operator rebooted the second and third hosts before recovery from the first had finished. Some PGs then had only one copy, below min_size. Bringing a host back restored IO. The team wrote a rule: set noout, one host at a time, wait for HEALTH_OK. Common mistakes Rebooting several storage hosts at once Ignoring nearfull until the cluster is full Running repair before understanding why a PG is inconsistent Forgetting to unset noout"},"ceph/capacity-planning-upgrades-and-operating-ceph":{"html":"\u003cp>The first deployment is the easy part. Day two is capacity forecasts, upgrades, hardware replacement and the habits that keep a storage cluster boring.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Raw, usable and safe capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading in the right order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expanding the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring and a day-two routine\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Usable capacity is raw capacity divided by the replication overhead, minus headroom. For three-way replication, 300 TB raw gives at most 100 TB usable. The cluster must also survive losing a failure domain: if one host of ten fails, the other nine must absorb its data. That means staying well below 90 percent. A common target is 70 to 75 percent used, with alerts at 65 and 75.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph df\nceph osd df tree\nceph osd pool autoscale-status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span>\nceph osd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> tree\nceph osd pool autoscale-status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Watch uneven OSD usage in \u003ccode>ceph osd df\u003c/code>. The \u003cstrong>balancer\u003c/strong> module moves PGs to even it out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrades\">Upgrades\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph orch upgrade start --ceph-version 18.2.4\nceph orch upgrade status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ceph orch upgrade start --ceph-version 18.2.4\nceph orch upgrade status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>cephadm upgrades monitors first, then managers, then OSDs, then MDS and RGW, one at a time, waiting for the cluster to be healthy between steps. Read the release notes, upgrade one major version at a time, and rehearse in a test cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"check&gt;change;change&gt;watch;watch&gt;next;cap&gt;change\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe change routine\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"check\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"change\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Make one change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"watch\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Watch recovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"next\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Next change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capacity forecast\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Check health to Make one change; 2, Make one change to Watch recovery; 3, Watch recovery to Next change; 4, Capacity forecast to Make one change.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"check\">\u003cdt>Check health \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cluster is HEALTH_OK before you start.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Starting on a warning hides new problems.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"change\">\u003cdt>Make one change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An upgrade step, a new host or a disk swap.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Several changes at once make faults hard to trace.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"watch\">\u003cdt>Watch recovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> PGs return to active+clean.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Client latency may rise during rebalancing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"next\">\u003cdt>Next change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Only after everything is clean.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping the wait is how outages start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cap\">\u003cdt>Capacity forecast \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tracks growth and the failure headroom.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Buying hardware late forces rushed changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expanding\">Expanding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add hosts and OSDs in steps. New OSDs trigger rebalancing, so add capacity before the cluster is full, and consider setting the upmap balancer so data moves gradually. Replacing old hardware follows the same path: add new, drain old, wait for clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routine\">Routine\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alerts on health state, capacity thresholds, slow ops, scrub errors, OSD flapping and clock skew. A written runbook for each. Quarterly: test a restore, a disk swap and an upgrade in a test cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-full-because-a-host-failed\">Incident: Full because a host failed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Writes stopped after a hardware failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ceph df\nceph health\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--- RAW STORAGE ---\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TOTAL  300 TiB  34 TiB  266 TiB   88.7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_ERR 3 full osd(s); pool(s) full\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>At 82 percent used the cluster had no room to rebuild a lost host. Recovery filled the surviving OSDs and writes stopped. The team added hosts, then set capacity alerts at 65 and 75 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Planning capacity for the healthy day only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading several components at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding hardware only when the cluster is nearly full\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alerts on capacity trends\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"upgrades","text":"Upgrades"},{"id":"expanding","text":"Expanding"},{"id":"routine","text":"Routine"},{"id":"incident-full-because-a-host-failed","text":"Incident: Full because a host failed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Upgrading Ceph with cephadm","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/cephadm/upgrade/"},{"t":"Ceph monitoring","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/mgr/prometheus/"},{"t":"Adding and removing OSDs","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/rados/operations/add-or-rm-osds/"}],"text":"The first deployment is the easy part. Day two is capacity forecasts, upgrades, hardware replacement and the habits that keep a storage cluster boring. What you will learn Raw, usable and safe capacity Upgrading in the right order Expanding the cluster Monitoring and a day-two routine Capacity Usable capacity is raw capacity divided by the replication overhead, minus headroom. For three-way replication, 300 TB raw gives at most 100 TB usable. The cluster must also survive losing a failure domain: if one host of ten fails, the other nine must absorb its data. That means staying well below 90 percent. A common target is 70 to 75 percent used, with alerts at 65 and 75. ceph df ceph osd df tree ceph osd pool autoscale-status Watch uneven OSD usage in ceph osd df . The balancer module moves PGs to even it out. Upgrades ceph orch upgrade start --ceph-version 18.2.4 ceph orch upgrade status cephadm upgrades monitors first, then managers, then OSDs, then MDS and RGW, one at a time, waiting for the cluster to be healthy between steps. Read the release notes, upgrade one major version at a time, and rehearse in a test cluster. A safe change routine Flow in order: 1, Check health to Make one change; 2, Make one change to Watch recovery; 3, Watch recovery to Next change; 4, Capacity forecast to Make one change. Observability Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Check health Observability What it does: Cluster is HEALTH_OK before you start. If it fails or is weak: Starting on a warning hides new problems. Make one change Step What it does: An upgrade step, a new host or a disk swap. If it fails or is weak: Several changes at once make faults hard to trace. Watch recovery Observability What it does: PGs return to active+clean. If it fails or is weak: Client latency may rise during rebalancing. Next change Step What it does: Only after everything is clean. If it fails or is weak: Skipping the wait is how outages start. Capacity forecast Data store What it does: Tracks growth and the failure headroom. If it fails or is weak: Buying hardware late forces rushed changes. Expanding Add hosts and OSDs in steps. New OSDs trigger rebalancing, so add capacity before the cluster is full, and consider setting the upmap balancer so data moves gradually. Replacing old hardware follows the same path: add new, drain old, wait for clean. Routine Alerts on health state, capacity thresholds, slow ops, scrub errors, OSD flapping and clock skew. A written runbook for each. Quarterly: test a restore, a disk swap and an upgrade in a test cluster. Incident: Full because a host failed Incident Writes stopped after a hardware failure. $ ceph df --- RAW STORAGE --- TOTAL 300 TiB 34 TiB 266 TiB 88.7 $ ceph health HEALTH_ERR 3 full osd(s); pool(s) full At 82 percent used the cluster had no room to rebuild a lost host. Recovery filled the surviving OSDs and writes stopped. The team added hosts, then set capacity alerts at 65 and 75 percent. Common mistakes Planning capacity for the healthy day only Upgrading several components at once Adding hardware only when the cluster is nearly full No alerts on capacity trends"}});